3. The server link's libraries: downloaded, never shipped
----------------------------------------------------------

DataPiler does not include these. At the first start, once the owner answers Y, DataPiler.ps1 has pip download them
from pypi.org into .venv in DataPiler's folder, each wheel checked against its sha256 in requirements.lock. Each
package's own licence files come with it, in its .dist-info folder inside .venv.

- grpcio 1.85.1: Apache License 2.0. Its licence file also carries a BSD 3-Clause licence (Copyright 2016,
  Google Inc.), for gRPC's Objective-C build files, and the Mozilla Public License 2.0, for the root certificates
  it ships (grpc/_cython/_credentials/roots.pem).
- protobuf 7.36.2: BSD 3-Clause licence (Copyright 2008 Google Inc.).
- typing-extensions 4.16.0: Python Software Foundation License Version 2 (PSF-2.0). grpcio needs it, so the lock
  names it too.

DataPiler imports them only in datapiler/link.py and soapstone/, and starts without them (no server link) when the
owner answers N.


4. The released DataPiler.exe: the MinGW-w64 runtime linked into it
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The repository holds no exe. The release workflow builds DataPiler.exe from launcher-src/ with MSYS2's MinGW-w64
GCC, linked -static, so parts of the toolchain's runtime are inside the exe:

- the mingw-w64 runtime (its CRT): under the Zope Public License 2.1, with parts in the public domain and parts
  under other permissive licences; ZPL 2.1 and several of those ask that their copyright notices and disclaimers
  go with a binary;
- winpthreads, which MSYS2's GCC (its posix thread model) links for the C++ runtime: under the MIT (Expat) licence,
  with parts under BSD 3-Clause licences, which ask the same;
- GCC's own runtime libraries (libgcc, libstdc++): under the GPL version 3 with the GCC Runtime Library Exception,
  which lets a program compiled with GCC be distributed under its own terms and asks for no notice.

The release workflow copies the licence files of these parts from the toolchain that built the exe into
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt and attaches that file to the release beside DataPiler.exe and its sha256. Those terms
cover the runtime parts only; DataPiler's own code in the exe stays under the MIT licence in LICENSE.
